// engine/effects/raster.emp — the sparse HInt raster dispatcher.
//
// Split out of engine/system/hblank.emp (which is dispatch MECHANISM only — the RAM
// jmp-slot trampoline) so the effects suite owns its own modules, per the design's
// file structure. Pure code motion: the program format, the timing model and every
// proc below are byte-for-byte what shipped in chain 105.
//
// Design: docs/superpowers/2026-08-11-effects-suite-design.md §4.
// Timing rules this is built on: docs/research/2026-08-12-raster-hint-survey.md.
module engine.effects.raster in raster

use engine.vdp.{VDP_HINT_OFF, vdp_comm, vdp_comm_addr, VdpTarget, VdpOp}
use engine.constants.{VDP_CTRL, VDP_DATA}
use engine.irq.{ints_off}

// ===========================================================================
// SPARSE RASTER DISPATCHER (effects P1) — the HBlank trampoline's first consumer.
//
// The SPARSE tier of the two-tier raster engine (design suite §4): a schedule of
// per-scanline VDP work in which HInt fires ONLY on event lines. The dense tier
// (gradient / per-column VSRAM runs) is Phase 2 and arrives as new opcodes.
//
// THE TIMING MODEL (docs/research/2026-08-12-raster-hint-survey.md, Ruling 1b —
// read it before touching this code). The VDP reloads its internal line counter
// from reg $0A *at the instant of underflow*, before the 68000 executes a single
// handler instruction. So a write to $8Axx from inside handler `i` is NOT consumed
// by the gap it is sitting in: it is consumed by the reload at fire `i+1`, and
// therefore schedules gap(i+1 -> i+2). Naive "write next_line - cur_line - 1" is
// off by a whole event.
//
// Two consequences shape the whole format:
//   1. Each fire record carries a BUILD-TIME arm word = $8A00 | (L[i+2]-L[i+1]-1).
//      The runtime never computes a delta and never compares a line number — the
//      schedule IS the program order. (Build-time computation over runtime.)
//   2. The program opens with TWO PRIMING records. Raster_VBlank leaves reg $0A = 0,
//      so cheap no-op fires land on lines 0 and 1 and load the first two real arm
//      words; every fire from the third onward lands exactly on its authored line.
//      Real events therefore start at line >= 2 — lines 0-1 belong to the priming
//      records. State that must cover them is settled state, set with Set_VDP_Reg.
//
// Fire lines are one line EARLY by construction: a register write in the handler
// for fire-line L takes effect from L+1 (Ruling 1a/3a), so an effect authored to
// begin at screen line M is scheduled at fire line M-1. The comptime constructors
// own that -1 so authors think in screen lines.
//
// A compiled raster program is DATA — the Phase-3 `raster_dsl` and Aurora both
// compile to exactly this layout:
//
//   Header — ONE word, so a fire record's arm word is always at word 1
//     dc.w pal_dirty_mask   bits 0-3: palette lines Raster_VBlank re-asserts into
//                           Palette_Dirty EVERY frame, so mid-frame CRAM writes are
//                           transient (the base palette is restored at frame top)
//
//   A program carries NO frame-top register words. It used to: every OP_SET_REG was
//   paired with a reset word in an `init[]` header block that Raster_VBlank replayed at
//   frame top. Flush_VDP_Shadow now re-blits ALL 19 shadowed VDP registers from
//   VDP_Shadow_Table unconditionally every VBlank, which restores every register a
//   mid-frame op touched, for free and without the program having to name it. Deleting
//   the mechanism is what lets two independently-authored effects touch the same register
//   and simply compose, instead of having to agree on a reset word to build at all.
//   A register change meant to SURVIVE the frame is settled state, not a raster op:
//   Set_VDP_Reg (engine/system/vdp_init.emp) writes the shadow and the flush delivers it.
//   Fire records, in FIRE ORDER (first two are the priming no-ops)
//     dc.w arm_word         $8A00 | delta — written at THIS fire, schedules the gap
//                           after next; RASTER_ARM_PARK on the last two real records
//     dc.w op_count         ops to run at this fire (0 = priming / no-op)
//     op_count x { dc.w op; ...args... }
//   Terminator
//     dc.w RASTER_ARM_PARK
//     dc.w RASTER_OPS_END   ($FFFF — park the counter and do NOT advance)
//
// Op argument layouts:
//   OP_SET_REG  dc.w $8xxx                  one VDP register word
//   OP_CRAM     dc.l <vdp_comm CRAM write>   full command longword (ONE move.l)
//               dc.w count-1                 count <= RASTER_CRAM_MAX
//               dc.w color[count]
//
// CYCLE BUDGET (Ruling 2a): ~105 68k cycles from HINT-pending to the next line's
// active display, less ~44 for autovector exception entry — about 60 usable. A CRAM
// write outside that window paints a visible dot (single-ported CRAM, Ruling 2b),
// which is why RASTER_CRAM_MAX is 3 and why the save set below is deliberately
// small: a 4-register movem round trip alone would be 40 of those cycles.
//
// VDP-access invariant (the HBlankHandler note above): the handler runs in raw
// interrupt context and writes the VDP control port, so it can corrupt a two-word
// command latch the main thread was mid-way through. Every main-loop VDP command
// pair reachable while a program is armed must sit inside an `ints_off` bracket.
// The handler itself only ever emits (a) single-word register writes and (b) full
// command longwords in ONE move.l, so it never leaves a split pair of its own.
// ===========================================================================

// ---- opcodes (compare-chain dispatch; see the note in Raster_HInt for why this
// is not a pc-indexed jump table) ----
pub const OP_SET_REG    = 0
pub const OP_CRAM       = 2
// OP_PAL_REGION (effects P2, Task 3) — a scanline REGION boundary: stream `count`
// pre-derived colour words from RAM to CRAM. Same 3-word wire body as OP_CRAM but the
// colours come from a RAM SOURCE POINTER (a variant's derived staging bytes) rather
// than being inline in the program. The derive happens at frame time in
// Palette_Compose (Palette_DeriveVariant -> Pal_Variant_Stage); the handler only
// streams, so it stays inside the ~60-cycle budget.
//
// WHAT A "REGION" IS (the RASTER_CRAM_MAX=3 ruling forces this choice, survey 2a):
// a full 16-colour line CANNOT swap in one fire (3 CRAM words/fire is a cycle budget,
// not a FIFO one). So a region is a SCOPED swap: the few visually-dominant entries of
// a line (<= RASTER_CRAM_MAX), streamed in one fire. A full-line region is authored as
// CONSECUTIVE OP_PAL_REGION fires on successive lines, so such a boundary takes ceil(N/3)
// lines to complete.
//
// THAT IS OUR CHOICE, NOT A REFERENCE'S — an earlier revision of this comment credited it
// to S3K, which is false and was corrected against the disassembly. S3K's HInt3
// (sonic3k.asm:1008-1046) swaps the whole water palette inside ONE interrupt: it parks the
// counter ($8AFF), stops the Z80, then loops {fresh CRAM command + 3 colours, then a
// cycle-counted ~370-cycle dbf spin}. The spacing that pushes the CRAM dots offscreen is
// the SPIN, not multiple interrupts. S3K's HInt2 (:1230), Sonic 2's PalToCRAM and S.C.E.'s
// HInt each write 64 CRAM words in a single fire with no delay at all. Ours trades six
// exception entries for a handler that stays inside the ~60-cycle budget with no Z80 stop
// and no long in-handler spin: EFX_BLANK_DELAY below is the same idea as S3K's spin at
// ~40 cycles instead of ~370, because it has to cover three words, not sixteen colours.
//
// The water cluster (Task 7) uses the scoped form: swap the handful of body colours at
// the water line, not the whole line.
pub const OP_PAL_REGION = 4

// pal_stage_off — the comptime offset into Pal_Variant_Stage an OP_PAL_REGION streams
// from: slot*128 + line*32 + entry*2. The variant staging is PAL_MAX_VARIANTS slots of
// a 128-byte 4-line image each (engine/ram.emp); Palette_DeriveVariant fills the covered
// lines at their natural line*32 offset. Kept as literals (128/32/2) rather than
// importing palette's consts to avoid an engine.effects.raster -> engine.effects.palette
// module edge for two numbers; the ensure() below pins the slot bound.
pub comptime fn pal_stage_off(slot: int, line: int, entry: int) -> int {
    ensure(slot >= 0 && slot < 2,     "pal_stage_off: slot {slot} outside 0..PAL_MAX_VARIANTS(2)")
    ensure(line >= 1 && line <= 3,    "pal_stage_off: line {line} outside 1..3 (line 0 is the character's)")
    ensure(entry >= 0 && entry <= 15, "pal_stage_off: entry {entry} outside 0..15")
    return slot * 128 + line * 32 + entry * 2
}

// OP_RUN_GRADIENT (effects P2, Task 5) — the DENSE tier. A dense run is a MODE, not an
// op that returns: for a line RANGE the handler fires EVERY line (reg $0A=0) and runs a
// minimal per-line body (write a constant CRAM command + 3 stream colours), then restores
// sparse delta dispatch at the end of the run. Wire body:
//   OP_RUN_GRADIENT
//   dc.l <CRAM write command longword>   constant target for every line (e.g. 3 sky entries)
//   dc.w <line count L>
//   dc.l <ROM gradient stream pointer>   L*3 colour words, 3 per line (pre-computed, no
//                                        handler arithmetic; Palette_Compose may refill it
//                                        when the gradient must follow the live palette)
pub const OP_RUN_GRADIENT = 6

// OP_RUN_RAMP — the dense tier's second body, and the first raster effect in this engine
// whose per-line values are COMPUTED rather than baked. Same run mechanics as
// OP_RUN_GRADIENT (fire every line, constant command, count down), but instead of reading
// three words per line from ROM it advances a 16.16 accumulator and writes its integer
// half. Wire body:
//   OP_RUN_RAMP
//   dc.l <VDP write command longword>    constant target for every line
//   dc.w <line count L>
//   dc.l <start>                         16.16 initial accumulator
//   dc.l <step>                          16.16 per-line delta, SIGNED
//
// WHY IT EXISTS. A gradient stream is frozen in ROM: it cannot change without rebuilding
// the data, so every dense effect was a fixture rather than a preset. The accumulator's
// two words live in RAM (Raster_Ramp_Acc / Raster_Ramp_Step) and a game may rewrite them
// on any frame, which is what turns "a gradient" into "a parameterised effect" — the
// dynamic-uniforms half the vocabulary review named as missing.
//
// Pointed at VSRAM this is vertical scale: each line scrolls the plane by a different
// amount, giving squash, stretch, and the perspective-floor family. Five reference
// engines independently converged on this exact 16.16-accumulator shape (Ristar's floor
// at ROM $061ACE does one divu per frame then add/asr per line; Vectorman's scaler at
// $0006F6F8 uses no table at all; also Gunstar, Alien Soldier and Thunder Force IV), and
// none of them could parameterise it from a data file the way this can.
pub const OP_RUN_RAMP = 8

// op_count sentinel: park the counter and do not advance the cursor. Reached only
// by a stray fire (a counter that somehow wrapped), so it is defense, not flow.
pub const RASTER_OPS_END = $FFFF

// reg $0A arm word that parks the counter past the end of the frame ($8A00|255):
// the next fire would be 256 lines away, i.e. never within active display.
pub const RASTER_ARM_PARK = $8AFF

// reg $0A arm word for the dense tier: value 0 = fire EVERY line (Task 5).
pub const RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE = $8A00

// Colors per OP_CRAM entry — a CYCLE budget (Ruling 2a), not a FIFO one.
pub const RASTER_CRAM_MAX = 3

// ---- row-119 partial-tint fix (Task 4) — DECIDED, measured on oracle 2026-08-13 ----
// The P1 residual: a CRAM write lands inside the fire+1 line's ACTIVE display, so pixels
// drawn after the write on that line carry the new colour (single-ported CRAM, survey
// Ruling 2b). Two options were built behind a select and measured at an IDENTICAL
// camera (2840, 356), section 1, both shapes converged:
//
//   (a) fire one line early — REJECTED and DELETED. It never removed the artifact by
//       construction (it relocates the partial row from M-1 to M-2 and starts the whole
//       effect a line high), and the audit found its helper `raster_fire_screen` was
//       imported but NEVER CALLED, so the option was dead code that had never affected
//       a shipped program. Numbers recorded in docs/benchmarks/effects-p2/GATE-EVIDENCE.md
//       so it is not re-litigated.
//   (b) blanking delay — ADOPTED, and now unconditional. A cycle-counted dbf before each
//       CRAM write parks it in HBlank (offscreen). Measured: row 119 goes 1 px -> 0 px
//       and the boundary lands on the AUTHORED line 120 instead of 119. S3K's approach
//       (sonic3k.asm:1018, :1038-1039, retuned per region, runtime-selected by measured
//       VBlank length at :9791-9793).
//
// RESIDUAL, deliberately not fixed here: the delay guards the CRAM paths only, so a fire
// mixing OP_SET_REG with a CRAM op still switches its mode REGISTER mid-line. Measured at
// ~45% across line 119 (column-bucket brightness, identical in both options). Authors
// wanting a pixel-clean mode change must schedule it a line earlier; extending the delay
// to OP_SET_REG would cost another ~40 cycles of a ~60-cycle budget and was not taken
// without a cycle measurement to justify it.
//
// dbf iterations. Each dbf is ~10 cyc; 4 covers the ~40 cyc from mid-handler to the
// active-display edge on an H40 line, and 4 is what the measurement above validates.
// Region-tunable — recalibrate on oracle if the handler's prologue changes (the ints_off
// bracket's stack push already shifted it once; see P1 GATE-EVIDENCE's CORRECTION).
const EFX_BLANK_DELAY = 4

// Working-buffer size (bytes). Buf_B is the Phase-2 patch/double-buffer seam;
// P1 runs entirely out of Buf_A.
pub const RASTER_BUF_SIZE = 128

// Raster_State span guard — the RAM block (engine/ram.emp) is the twin of this
// layout; a drift on either side must fail the build, not the frame.
//   Program 4 + Cursor 4 + Pending 4 + Line 2 + Buf_A + Buf_B + Active_Buf 4
//   + Dense_Lines 2 + Dense_Cursor 4 + Dense_Cmd 4 + Water_Line 2 + Water_World_Y 2
//   + Dense_Kind 2 + Ramp_Acc 4 + Ramp_Step 4
pub const RASTER_STATE_SIZE = 4 + 4 + 4 + 2 + RASTER_BUF_SIZE + RASTER_BUF_SIZE + 4 + 2 + 4 + 4 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 4 + 4
ensure((extern("Raster_State_End") - extern("Raster_State")) == RASTER_STATE_SIZE,
       "Raster_State RAM block drifted from hblank.emp's raster layout")

// ---- comptime authoring helpers -------------------------------------------
// What survives here is the arm arithmetic the DENSE tier still authors with: the
// arm word at record i schedules gap(i+1 -> i+2). The SPARSE tier's authoring
// surface — including the other off-by-one, `fire line = screen line - 1` — is
// engine.effects.raster_dsl, which owns the general constructors and their
// validation. Helpers this module no longer needs have been deleted, not kept
// dormant (raster_fire_screen at the row-119 note above set that precedent).

// raster_arm — the arm word to place in record i, given the FIRE lines of records
// i+1 and i+2. Pass RASTER_ARM_PARK's operand (255) when there is no i+2.
pub comptime fn raster_arm(next_fire: int, after_fire: int) -> int {
    ensure(after_fire > next_fire,
           "raster_arm: fire lines must strictly increase ({next_fire} -> {after_fire})")
    ensure(after_fire - next_fire - 1 <= 255,
           "raster_arm: gap {next_fire}->{after_fire} exceeds one counter reload")
    return $8A00 | (after_fire - next_fire - 1)
}

// ---- dense-run authoring (Task 5) -----------------------------------------
// RasterGradientProgram — the wire shape of a minimal dense-run program: two priming
// records, the setup record carrying OP_RUN_GRADIENT, and the terminator. A struct
// rather than the flat [u16] the sparse fixtures use, because the gradient op carries a
// LINK-TIME stream pointer — OP_PAL_REGION deliberately avoided one by carrying a
// comptime staging offset instead, but a ROM stream has no staging base to be relative
// to, and a [u16] literal array cannot hold a symbol address.
//
// THE ENTER SCHEDULE, for a run of L lines whose first stream line lands on screen line
// T (the other half of the mode-switch schedule commented at the dense fast path):
//   fire 0 @ line 0     priming, arm = raster_arm(1, T-1)
//   fire 1 @ line 1     priming, arm = raster_arm(T-1, T) = the every-line word
//   fire 2 @ line T-1   setup: OP_RUN_GRADIENT stashes cmd/lines/cursor, writes nothing
//   fire 3 @ line T     first .dense_body -> stream[0] shows on screen line T
//   ...                 L dense fires; the last stream line lands on screen line T+L-1
// Record i's arm governs gap(i+1 -> i+2), so BOTH record 1 and record 2 must carry the
// every-line word: record 1's puts fire 3 at T, record 2's puts fire 4 at T+1. The
// setup fire writes no colours of its own, which is why it gets its own line at T-1.
//
// THE T-1 SETUP LINE IS MEASURED, NOT DERIVED. The naive derivation puts the setup at
// T-2 (fire 3 then lands at T-1, whose handler write shows on T, per the sparse tier's
// fire-line = M-1 rule). On hardware that authors the whole run ONE LINE HIGH: with
// top=96 the level boundaries came out at 107/119/131/143/155/167/179 instead of
// 108/120/.../180 — a uniform -1 across all seven, i.e. stream[0] displayed on 95.
// The dense path does not inherit the sparse -1 because entering the run costs its own
// pipelined arm (Ruling 1b at the ENTER edge), which absorbs exactly one line. Evidence:
// docs/benchmarks/effects-p2/GATE-EVIDENCE.md, dense-tier section.
pub struct RasterGradientProgram {
    rgp_mask:       u16,    // pal_dirty_mask — DERIVED from rgp_cmd's CRAM address
    rgp_arm0:       u16, rgp_ops0: u16,     // fire 0 — priming
    rgp_arm1:       u16, rgp_ops1: u16,     // fire 1 — priming
    rgp_arm2:       u16, rgp_ops2: u16,     // fire 2 — the setup record, one op
    rgp_op:         u16,
    rgp_cmd:        u32,    // constant CRAM write command, re-issued every dense line
    rgp_lines:      u16,
    rgp_stream:     *u8,    // rgp_lines * RASTER_CRAM_MAX colour words
    rgp_end_arm:    u16, rgp_end_ops: u16,  // terminator
}

// raster_gradient_program — build the schedule above. `cmd` is the CRAM write command
// longword, passed in already built (game-side, pinned against engine.vdp's comptime
// builder).
//
// THE MASK IS DERIVED, NOT ACCEPTED. It used to be a parameter, and it was the last
// hand-authorable pal_dirty_mask in the suite — the one field with a shipped history of
// producing wrong pixels (P1: the mask said %0001 while the op wrote line 2, so the red
// covered the whole ground instead of the band, permanently). The sparse tier has always
// derived it (raster_dsl's op_mask); the dense tier took the author's word for it. Now
// both derive from the same fact — the CRAM address inside the command longword — so a
// mask that disagrees with the write is UNREPRESENTABLE rather than merely detectable.
//
// `stream_words` is the array's actual length, checked against the run's demand. The
// alternative was a co-located hand-written ensure at each call site (the shipped fixture
// carried one), which every future dense program would have to remember to copy — the
// ritual this codebase keeps discovering it has not copied.
//
// THERE IS NO `init_word` PARAMETER ANY MORE. A dense program used to carry one frame-top
// register word (defaulting to boot's H40 base $8C81) that Raster_VBlank replayed; the
// unconditional Flush_VDP_Shadow now restores every shadowed register at frame top, so the
// header word had nothing left to do. A section that wants a register CHANGED globally —
// Shadow/Highlight permanently on, say — calls Set_VDP_Reg (engine/system/vdp_init.emp)
// instead: it writes the shadow byte, and the frame-top flush is what delivers it.
pub comptime fn raster_gradient_program(top: int, lines: int, cmd: int,
                                        stream: Label, stream_words: int) -> RasterGradientProgram {
    // Floor is 3, matching `raster_dsl.fire`: the setup fire sits at T-1 and must still
    // land after the two priming fires on lines 0-1.
    ensure(top >= 3, "raster_gradient_program: top {top} below 3 (priming needs lines 0-1)")
    ensure(lines >= 1, "raster_gradient_program: line count {lines} must be positive")
    ensure(top + lines <= 224,
           "raster_gradient_program: run {top}..{top + lines - 1} runs past screen line 223")
    // The command must BE a CRAM write, or the derived mask is a palette line computed
    // from something that is not a palette address. The round trip proves target, op and
    // address in one comparison, and it subsumes the pin each call site used to carry.
    let addr = vdp_comm_addr(cmd)
    ensure(cmd == vdp_comm(addr, VdpTarget.Cram, VdpOp.Write),
           "raster_gradient_program: cmd {cmd} is not a CRAM WRITE command — the dense run streams colour words to CRAM and derives its pal_dirty_mask from this address, so a VRAM or VSRAM command here would both write the wrong place and re-assert a palette line computed from a non-palette address")
    ensure(addr <= 126,
           "raster_gradient_program: CRAM byte address {addr} outside 0..126")
    ensure((addr >> 5) != 0,
           "raster_gradient_program: the run writes CRAM line 0, the CHARACTER's palette line — a dense run re-asserts its line every frame, so this would fight the player's palette for the whole section")
    ensure(stream_words == lines * RASTER_CRAM_MAX,
           "raster_gradient_program: stream is {stream_words} words but the run needs lines * RASTER_CRAM_MAX = {lines * RASTER_CRAM_MAX}. A short stream is read past its end into whatever ROM follows, for the rest of the run, every frame.")
    return RasterGradientProgram{
        rgp_mask:       1 << (addr >> 5),
        rgp_arm0:       raster_arm(1, top - 1),
        rgp_ops0:       0,
        rgp_arm1:       raster_arm(top - 1, top),       // == RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE
        rgp_ops1:       0,
        rgp_arm2:       RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE,
        rgp_ops2:       1,
        rgp_op:         OP_RUN_GRADIENT,
        rgp_cmd:        cmd,
        rgp_lines:      lines,
        rgp_stream:     stream,
        rgp_end_arm:    RASTER_ARM_PARK,
        rgp_end_ops:    RASTER_OPS_END,
    }
}

// RasterRampProgram — the wire shape of a dense RAMP program. Same schedule as
// RasterGradientProgram (two priming records, a setup record carrying the op, then the
// terminator); the body differs only in what the setup record carries.
pub struct RasterRampProgram {
    rrp_mask:       u16,    // pal_dirty_mask — see the constructor; 0 for a non-CRAM target
    rrp_arm0:       u16, rrp_ops0: u16,     // fire 0 — priming
    rrp_arm1:       u16, rrp_ops1: u16,     // fire 1 — priming
    rrp_arm2:       u16, rrp_ops2: u16,     // fire 2 — the setup record, one op
    rrp_op:         u16,
    rrp_cmd:        u32,    // constant VDP write command, re-issued every line
    rrp_lines:      u16,
    rrp_start:      u32,    // 16.16 initial accumulator
    rrp_step:       u32,    // 16.16 per-line delta, signed
    rrp_end_arm:    u16, rrp_end_ops: u16,  // terminator
}

// raster_ramp_program — a dense run whose per-line value is COMPUTED from a 16.16
// accumulator instead of streamed from ROM, which is what makes it parameterisable at
// runtime (Raster_Ramp_Acc / Raster_Ramp_Step are RAM).
//
// `start` and `step` are 16.16 FIXED POINT: 65536 is one unit per line. Authors should
// use fp16() below rather than writing the raw longword, so the scale is stated once.
//
// WHICH LINE THE FIRST VALUE LANDS ON DEPENDS ON THE TARGET, and it is MEASURED, not
// assumed (docs/benchmarks/effects-p3/RAMP-EVIDENCE.md, 74 of 74 rows):
//   CRAM target  — value j displays on screen line top + j     (like the gradient tier)
//   VSRAM target — value j displays on screen line top + j + 1
// The extra line is the same N+1 behaviour measured for a sparse `vsram` write on
// 2026-08-14: a VSRAM write issued in line N's HBlank takes effect on N+1, while CRAM
// applies to the line being set up. So a VSRAM ramp's `top` line renders with whatever
// scroll was live BEFORE the run, and the ramp proper starts one line down.
//
// This is deliberately NOT corrected by biasing `top` inside the constructor. The bias
// would have to be conditional on the target, which puts a silent one-line shift between
// two calls that differ only in their command word — the exact class of surprise the
// sparse tier's single `-1` rule was designed to avoid. Author the line you want the
// ramp to START at and, for VSRAM, expect the line above it to hold the previous value.
//
// THE MASK IS DERIVED, exactly as the gradient constructor derives it, and for the same
// reason — a pal_dirty_mask naming the wrong line leaves a mid-frame write latched
// forever. A ramp pointed at VSRAM writes no palette, so its mask is 0; that is the same
// ruling `vsram`'s op_mask carries in the sparse tier, and it is NOT an omission: a
// CRAM-style `1 << (addr >> 5)` computed from a VSRAM address is a palette line derived
// from a scroll offset, and at offset 0 it names the CHARACTER's line.
//
// THERE IS NO `init_word` PARAMETER ANY MORE, for the same reason the gradient constructor
// lost its one: Flush_VDP_Shadow re-blits every shadowed register from VDP_Shadow_Table at
// the top of every VBlank, so a program-carried frame-top word is redundant. A section
// wanting a globally-changed register (Shadow/Highlight permanently on, for instance)
// calls Set_VDP_Reg (engine/system/vdp_init.emp) and lets the flush deliver it.
pub comptime fn raster_ramp_program(top: int, lines: int, cmd: int,
                                    start: int, step: int) -> RasterRampProgram {
    ensure(top >= 3, "raster_ramp_program: top {top} below 3 (priming needs lines 0-1)")
    ensure(lines >= 1, "raster_ramp_program: line count {lines} must be positive")
    ensure(top + lines <= 224,
           "raster_ramp_program: run {top}..{top + lines - 1} runs past screen line 223")
    let addr = vdp_comm_addr(cmd)
    // Exactly one of the two supported targets, and the mask follows from which. INT
    // flags, not booleans, and the mask is built in a comptime var rather than an
    // if-expression: `if` is a statement here, and a value-position `if` in a struct
    // literal resolves as a LABEL rather than failing loudly (the silent-degradation
    // trap this module's opening note warns about — hit while writing this function).
    comptime var is_cram  = 0
    comptime var is_vsram = 0
    if cmd == vdp_comm(addr, VdpTarget.Cram,  VdpOp.Write) { is_cram  = 1 }
    if cmd == vdp_comm(addr, VdpTarget.Vsram, VdpOp.Write) { is_vsram = 1 }
    ensure(is_cram + is_vsram == 1,
           "raster_ramp_program: cmd {cmd} is neither a CRAM nor a VSRAM write command. A ramp re-issues this command every line and derives its pal_dirty_mask from the target, so an unrecognised one would both write the wrong place and re-assert a palette line computed from a non-palette address.")
    ensure(is_vsram == 0 || addr <= 78,
           "raster_ramp_program: VSRAM byte address {addr} outside 0..78")
    ensure(is_cram == 0 || addr <= 126,
           "raster_ramp_program: CRAM byte address {addr} outside 0..126")
    ensure(is_cram == 0 || (addr >> 5) != 0,
           "raster_ramp_program: the run writes CRAM line 0, the CHARACTER's palette line — a dense run re-asserts its line every frame, so this would fight the player's palette for the whole section")
    comptime var mask = 0
    if is_cram == 1 { mask = 1 << (addr >> 5) }
    return RasterRampProgram{
        rrp_mask:       mask,
        rrp_arm0:       raster_arm(1, top - 1),
        rrp_ops0:       0,
        rrp_arm1:       raster_arm(top - 1, top),       // == RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE
        rrp_ops1:       0,
        rrp_arm2:       RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE,
        rrp_ops2:       1,
        rrp_op:         OP_RUN_RAMP,
        rrp_cmd:        cmd,
        rrp_lines:      lines,
        rrp_start:      start,
        rrp_step:       step,
        rrp_end_arm:    RASTER_ARM_PARK,
        rrp_end_ops:    RASTER_OPS_END,
    }
}

// fp16 — build a 16.16 fixed-point value from whole and 1/256 parts, so a ramp's rate is
// authored as "pixels per line" rather than as a raw longword nobody can read.
//   fp16(1, 0)   = +1 px per line          fp16(0, 128) = +0.5 px per line
//   fp16(-1, 0)  = -1 px per line
pub comptime fn fp16(whole: int, frac256: int) -> int {
    ensure(frac256 >= 0 && frac256 <= 255, "fp16: frac256 {frac256} outside 0..255")
    ensure(whole >= -512 && whole <= 511, "fp16: whole {whole} outside -512..511")
    if whole < 0 { return (whole * 65536) - (frac256 * 256) }
    return (whole * 65536) + (frac256 * 256)
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_Install — stage a compiled program; it takes effect at the next
// Raster_VBlank, so an install can never tear a frame mid-walk.
//   a0 = program (0 = clear/uninstall)
// Main-loop context only.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_Install (a0: u32) clobbers() {
        move.l  a0, Raster_Pending
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_VBlank — once per frame from VInt_Level AND VInt_Lag.
//
// MUST run BEFORE Flush_VDP_Shadow: HBlank_Install arms reg $0A through the VDP
// shadow, so the arm only reaches the hardware on THIS frame's flush
// (unconditional, every register, every frame — see engine/system/vdp_init.emp)
// — a post-flush position would delay every arm a frame (Ruling 1d).
// It also ORs the program's pal_dirty_mask into Palette_Dirty, which must land
// before Enqueue_Dirty_Buffers reads the mask — Flush precedes Enqueue, so a
// pre-Flush position satisfies both orderings.
//
// Consumes a pending install, re-asserts the program's palette lines, rewinds the
// cursor to the first (priming) record, and leaves reg $0A = 0 so the pipeline
// primes on lines 0 and 1. Frame-top REGISTER state is not its job — the
// unconditional Flush_VDP_Shadow immediately after does that for all 19 registers.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_VBlank () clobbers(d0-d2/a0-a2) {
        move.l  Raster_Pending, d0
        beq.s   .no_install
        clr.l   Raster_Pending
        move.l  d0, Raster_Program
        bne.s   .copy_program
        // Pending was an explicit clear: idle the trampoline and drop IE1.
        jbsr    HBlank_Uninstall
        rts
    .copy_program:
        movea.l d0, a1
        lea     Raster_Buf_A, a2
        move.l  a2, Raster_Active_Buf
        move.w  #(RASTER_BUF_SIZE / 2) - 1, d1
    .copy:
        move.w  (a1)+, (a2)+
        dbf     d1, .copy

    .no_install:
        move.l  Raster_Program, d0
        beq.s   .done                   // no program armed: one tst per frame
        movea.l Raster_Active_Buf, a1
        // --- header ---
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // pal_dirty_mask
        or.b    d1, Palette_Dirty       // re-assert the base palette every frame
        // The whole header. There is no frame-top register replay here any more:
        // Flush_VDP_Shadow runs right after this and re-blits every shadowed VDP
        // register unconditionally, which restores anything a mid-frame op changed.
        move.l  a1, Raster_Cursor       // first (priming) fire record
        // reg $0A = 0 primes the pipeline: no-op fires on lines 0 and 1 load the
        // first two real arm words. Re-installed every frame on purpose — it is
        // idempotent and it heals the counter the last record parked at $8AFF.
        moveq   #0, d0
        // PC-relative, not absolute: HBlank_Install stores this into the RAM jmp slot,
        // so a0 must end up holding Raster_HInt's true runtime address — which
        // `lea (pc)` yields just as well as an absolute operand, while keeping this
        // module's emitted bytes PLACEMENT-INVARIANT. That invariance is a property
        // the port harness probes (hblank_negative_probes' wrong-base probe asserts
        // the same section linked at two bases carries identical bytes), so an
        // absolute self-reference here would break a test for no gain.
        lea     Raster_HInt(pc), a0
        jbsr    HBlank_Install
    .done:
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_HInt — the IRQ4 handler, entered by `jmp` from HBlank_Vector_Slot.
//
// Interrupt-transparent per the HBlankHandler contract: observable clobbers are
// EMPTY, the movem round trip is declared by preserves(), and it ends in `rte`.
//
// Per fire: publish this record's build-time arm word (FIRST — it is the one write
// that must not be lost, and it schedules gap(i+1 -> i+2) per Ruling 1b), then run
// this record's ops, then advance the cursor. No line compares, no delta math.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_HInt () clobbers() preserves(d0-d1/a1-a2) {
        // Raise to IPL 7 for the body. The 68000 enters IRQ4 at IPL 4, so IRQ6
        // (VBlank) can nest inside this handler — and a nested VBlank between an
        // OP_CRAM command longword and the colour words that follow it would
        // retarget the VDP address latch, sending our colours wherever VBlank left
        // it. Precedent: every long HInt payload in TF4 / Vectorman / Gunstar /
        // Alien Soldier opens by raising the mask (survey Q4).
        //
        // The engine's `ints_off` bracket rather than a bare `move.w #$2700,sr`:
        // bare, the write needs `sr` in the contract, and adding it to preserves()
        // defeats the movem round-trip proof (the checker cannot model `rte`
        // restoring SR, so the whole clause stops verifying and every saved
        // register reports undeclared). The bracket declares its own effect.
        //
        // `rte` MUST sit outside the bracket. Order: acquire pushes SR, the movem
        // pushes the registers, ... movem pops them, release pops SR, and only then
        // does `rte` pop the exception frame the CPU pushed. Balanced. An `rte`
        // INSIDE the bracket would leave the pushed SR word on the stack and `rte`
        // would pop a frame shifted by one word.
        with ints_off {
        movem.l d0-d1/a1-a2, -(sp)
        lea     VDP_CTRL, a2
        // -------------------------------------------------------------------
        // DENSE-RUN FAST PATH (Task 5). While a gradient run is active the handler
        // bypasses the record walk: it fires every line, writes the constant CRAM
        // command + 3 stream colours, advances the cursor, and re-arms reg $0A=0.
        //
        // THE MODE-SWITCH SCHEDULE (both transitions, per Ruling 1b — the one-interrupt
        // arm lag). reg $0A is a CONSTANT 0 across the whole run, so there is no per-line
        // delta to pipeline; the lag only bites at the two edges:
        //   ENTER: the fire record carrying OP_RUN_GRADIENT is authored with arm word 0
        //     (raster_arm gap 1). That 0, written at the setup fire, is consumed at the
        //     NEXT fire's reload -> the first dense line fires one line after setup.
        //
        //     CORRECTED 2026-08-14. This comment used to conclude "author the fire at
        //     gradient_top - 2", which is the NAIVE derivation the hardware REJECTED:
        //     it authors the whole run one line high (with top=96 the seven ramp
        //     boundaries came out at 107/119/.../179 instead of 108/120/.../180). The
        //     measured setup line is T-1, not T-2 — see the T-1 note at the top of the
        //     dense-tier section, which records the measurement and why the dense path
        //     does not inherit the sparse -1. raster_gradient_program has always used
        //     T-1 (`rgp_arm1: raster_arm(top - 1, top)`); only this comment was stale,
        //     and it sat in the module docs/EFFECTS_AUTHORING.md names as the runtime
        //     source of truth. Anyone hand-authoring a dense program from here would
        //     have reproduced the exact bug the P2 gate already caught once.
        //   LEAVE: the LAST dense fire (Raster_Dense_Lines 1->0) still writes reg $0A=0,
        //     so one extra every-line fire happens after the run; that fire finds
        //     Dense_Lines==0, falls through to the record walk at Raster_Cursor (already
        //     past the gradient op), and writes the NEXT record's arm word. Because of
        //     the lag that arm governs the fire after next, so the first post-gradient
        //     sparse event must be scheduled >= 2 lines below the run's last line. The
        //     comptime authoring helper owns that gap; the run body does not.
        // Conservative register model: the cursor is reloaded from Raster_Dense_Cursor
        // (RAM) every fire and only d0-d1/a1-a2 are saved. A reserved global stream
        // register (Gunstar's a6 / Alien Soldier's zero-save handler, survey Ruling 4c)
        // would drop the reload but changes engine-wide register conventions — a FLAGGED
        // decision needing user sign-off (see the report), not taken here.
        // -------------------------------------------------------------------
        tst.w   Raster_Dense_Lines
        bne     .dense_body
        movea.l Raster_Cursor, a1
        move.w  (a1)+, (a2)             // arm word -> reg $0A (consumed at fire i+1)
        move.w  (a1)+, d0               // op_count
        cmpi.w  #RASTER_OPS_END, d0
        beq     .park                   // terminator: stray fire, do not advance
        subq.w  #1, d0
        bmi     .advance                // priming / no-op record (unsized: OP_RUN_RAMP's
                                        // ENTER arm pushed this past a short displacement)
    .op_loop:
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // op
        // Three-op compare chain rather than a pc-indexed jump table: at this few
        // opcodes it is fewer cycles than the table setup, and it keeps the handler
        // analyzable — a computed `jmp` defeats the contract-closure dataflow, and
        // proc.clobber-undeclared is zero-firing by contract. Frequency-ordered:
        // OP_CRAM (the common raster write) first, then OP_PAL_REGION (region
        // boundaries), OP_SET_REG the fall-through. The opcodes stay byte offsets so a
        // table can drop back in without touching the program format.
        cmpi.w  #OP_CRAM, d1
        beq.s   .op_cram
        cmpi.w  #OP_PAL_REGION, d1
        beq     .op_region
        cmpi.w  #OP_RUN_GRADIENT, d1
        beq     .op_run_gradient
        cmpi.w  #OP_RUN_RAMP, d1
        beq     .op_run_ramp
        // OP_SET_REG — one $8xxx word
        move.w  (a1)+, (a2)
        dbf     d0, .op_loop
        jbra    .advance
    .op_cram:
        move.l  (a1)+, (a2)             // full CRAM command in ONE move.l (sets the address)
        // Row-119 fix: burn a cycle-counted delay so the colour writes below land in
        // HBlank (offscreen). d1 is free here, before it is loaded with count.
        moveq   #EFX_BLANK_DELAY, d1
    .cram_delay:
        dbf     d1, .cram_delay
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // count-1
    .cram_loop:
        move.w  (a1)+, VDP_DATA
        dbf     d1, .cram_loop
        dbf     d0, .op_loop
        jbra    .advance
    .op_region:
        // Stream count pre-derived colour words from a variant's Pal_Variant_Stage
        // bytes to CRAM. The program carries a comptime OFFSET into Pal_Variant_Stage
        // (slot*128 + line*32 + entry*2 — pal_stage_off below), NOT a link-time
        // pointer, so the whole program stays a flat [u16] of comptime literals. a2
        // (VDP_CTRL) is repurposed as the source cursor (colours go to VDP_DATA
        // absolute, which needs no a2), then restored for the arm write / next op — no
        // register is added to the movem save set (the ~60-cycle budget).
        move.l  (a1)+, (a2)             // CRAM write command longword (a2 = VDP_CTRL)
        // Row-119 fix: same blanking delay as OP_CRAM (d1 free before count).
        moveq   #EFX_BLANK_DELAY, d1
    .region_delay:
        dbf     d1, .region_delay
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // count-1  (<= RASTER_CRAM_MAX)
        lea     Pal_Variant_Stage, a2   // staging base (was VDP_CTRL)
        adda.w  (a1)+, a2               // + comptime offset -> the derived colours
    .region_loop:
        move.w  (a2)+, VDP_DATA
        dbf     d1, .region_loop
        lea     VDP_CTRL, a2            // restore the control-port cursor
        dbf     d0, .op_loop
        jbra    .advance
    .op_run_gradient:
        // ENTER a dense run: stash the constant command, the line count, and the ROM
        // stream cursor into RAM, then fall through to .advance (Raster_Cursor now points
        // past the op, where the sparse walk resumes when the run ends). Every line of the
        // run is written by .dense_body on the subsequent every-line fires. Must be the
        // LAST op in its record (the record's arm word is authored 0 so the pipeline
        // starts firing every line — see the ENTER schedule at the handler top).
        move.l  (a1)+, Raster_Dense_Cmd     // constant CRAM write command
        move.w  (a1)+, Raster_Dense_Lines   // L lines
        move.l  (a1)+, Raster_Dense_Cursor  // ROM stream pointer (L*3 words)
        clr.w   Raster_Dense_Kind           // 0 = stream the ROM gradient
        // fall through to .advance
        jbra    .advance
    .op_run_ramp:
        // ENTER a dense RAMP run. Same shape as the gradient ENTER — stash the constant
        // command and the line count, fall through to .advance — but seed the accumulator
        // from the program instead of a ROM cursor. The seed is deliberate: re-entering a
        // section restarts the ramp from its authored start rather than wherever the last
        // run left the accumulator, so the effect is not history-dependent.
        move.l  (a1)+, Raster_Dense_Cmd     // constant VDP write command (VSRAM or CRAM)
        move.w  (a1)+, Raster_Dense_Lines   // L lines
        move.l  (a1)+, Raster_Ramp_Acc      // 16.16 start
        move.l  (a1)+, Raster_Ramp_Step     // 16.16 per-line delta (signed)
        move.w  #1, Raster_Dense_Kind       // 1 = compute
        // fall through to .advance
    .advance:
        move.l  a1, Raster_Cursor
        jbra    .out
    .dense_body:
        // One dense line: keep firing every line, write the constant command + 3 stream
        // colours, advance the cursor, count down. When Raster_Dense_Lines reaches 0 the
        // NEXT fire finds it clear and resumes the sparse record walk at Raster_Cursor
        // (LEAVE schedule at the handler top). Conservative: cursor reloaded from RAM,
        // no reserved register.
        move.w  #RASTER_ARM_EVERY_LINE, (a2)       // reg $0A=0: fire every line
        move.l  Raster_Dense_Cmd, (a2)             // constant command (sets the address)
        // Which body? Both dense kinds share the every-line arm and the constant command
        // above, and differ only in where the per-line words come from. One compare, on
        // the shared prologue, rather than two near-duplicate bodies each re-arming and
        // re-issuing the command.
        tst.w   Raster_Dense_Kind
        bne.s   .ramp_body
        movea.l Raster_Dense_Cursor, a1
        move.w  (a1)+, VDP_DATA
        move.w  (a1)+, VDP_DATA
        move.w  (a1)+, VDP_DATA
        move.l  a1, Raster_Dense_Cursor
        subq.w  #1, Raster_Dense_Lines
        jbra    .out
    .ramp_body:
        // One ramp line: advance the 16.16 accumulator and emit its INTEGER half. `swap`
        // is the whole trick — it moves the high word down with no shift loop, so the
        // fixed-point divide is free. This is Ristar's per-line body (add, shift, store)
        // with the shift folded into the word select.
        move.l  Raster_Ramp_Acc, d1
        add.l   Raster_Ramp_Step, d1
        move.l  d1, Raster_Ramp_Acc
        swap    d1                                 // high word = integer part
        move.w  d1, VDP_DATA
        subq.w  #1, Raster_Dense_Lines
        jbra    .out
    .park:
        move.w  #RASTER_ARM_PARK, (a2)
    .out:
        movem.l (sp)+, d0-d1/a1-a2
        }
        rte
}

// The canonical "explicitly no raster effects here" program: no palette
// re-assert and a terminator that parks the counter. Installing it is how a
// section turns a neighbour's effect OFF.
pub data Raster_Program_None: [u16; 3] = [0, RASTER_ARM_PARK, RASTER_OPS_END]

// ===========================================================================
// WATER_LEVEL PATCH SLOT (effects P2 Task 7) — a raster program whose boundary line
// MOVES at runtime without rebuilding the whole program. The back buffer Raster_Buf_B
// (reserved by P1 for exactly this) holds a runtime COPY of a template whose priming
// arm word is recomputed from Raster_Water_Line; Raster_VBlank walks it because
// Raster_Active_Buf points at Buf_B. Only the arm word moves, so a line change is one
// word write.
//
// The header is ONE word, so priming record 0's arm word sits at byte offset 2 in EVERY
// program — there is no longer a layout precondition on the template:
//   [pal_dirty_mask 0][arm0 2][opc0 4][arm1 6][opc1 8]...
// It used to be offset 6, valid only while a template carried exactly one frame-top init
// word ([mask 0][init_count 2][init[0] 4][arm0 6]). The init header is gone — the blanket
// Flush_VDP_Shadow restores every register at frame top — so what was a coupling an
// author could break is now structural.
pub const WATER_TEMPLATE_ARM0_OFF = 2

// Raster_WaterArm — the priming arm word that lands the water fire at screen line M.
// FORMULA (survey Ruling 1b — the one-interrupt arm lag): priming fires 0 and 1 land on
// lines 0 and 1 (Raster_VBlank leaves reg $0A=0); the arm word WRITTEN at priming record
// 0 is consumed at the NEXT reload and schedules gap(1 -> event). The event fire must be
// at M-1 so its writes land on M (Ruling 1a). Priming fire 1 is at line 1, so the gap
// 1 -> (M-1) needs reg $0A = (M-1) - 1 - 1 = M - 3. So arm0 = $8A00 | (M - 3).
//
// RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE is the `- 3` above, named ONCE: engine.effects.raster_dsl's
// arm_at reduces to $8A00 | (M - 3) for a single-event program and pins itself against
// this constant; Raster_PatchWaterLine below is the runtime twin. They were previously
// two hand-synced literals with only a comment holding them together.
pub const RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE = 3
pub const RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE = 223

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_CopyPatchedTemplate — shared prologue for both patched-template install
// paths (screen-line and world-Y): copy the template into Raster_Buf_B and make it
// the live program. Bypasses Raster_Pending (which drives the ROM-program Buf_A
// path) — the water program is the runtime-patched Buf_B, not a static ROM program.
//   a0 = patched template
// -----------------------------------------------
proc Raster_CopyPatchedTemplate (a0: u32) clobbers(d1/a0-a1) {
        lea     Raster_Buf_B, a1
        move.l  a1, Raster_Active_Buf
        move.l  a1, Raster_Program              // nonzero -> Raster_VBlank processes it
        clr.l   Raster_Pending
        move.w  #(RASTER_BUF_SIZE / 2) - 1, d1
    .copy:
        move.w  (a0)+, (a1)+
        dbf     d1, .copy
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_InstallWater — install a water template into Raster_Buf_B and make it the
// live program, with the boundary at screen line d0.
//   a0 = water template   d0.w = water screen line
// After this, Raster_VBlank walks Buf_B every frame; Raster_PatchWaterLine moves the
// line.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_InstallWater (a0: u32, d0: u16) clobbers(d0-d1/a0-a1) {
        jbsr    Raster_CopyPatchedTemplate
        // Seed the AUTHORED world Y from the screen line the caller asked for, so an
        // install still reads as "put the boundary here on screen" while every frame
        // after it is world-anchored. world_y = screen_line + Camera_Y(int).
        move.w  Camera_Y, d1
        add.w   d1, d0
        move.w  d0, Raster_Water_World_Y
        jbra    Raster_PatchWaterWorldY
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_InstallPatchedWorldY — install a patched template into Raster_Buf_B and make
// it the live program, with the boundary at an AUTHORED world Y.
//   a0 = patched template   d0.w = authored world Y
//
// NOT Raster_InstallWater with a different argument. A preset authors its patched
// boundary as a world Y — a property of the level, not the screen. Raster_InstallWater
// takes a SCREEN line and DERIVES world Y by adding Camera_Y (so an install still reads
// as "put the boundary here on screen" while every frame after it is world-anchored).
// Feeding an already-authored world Y through that same derivation would store
// world_y + Camera_Y: wrong on install, and differently wrong on every re-entry
// depending on where the camera happens to be at that moment. This entry point stores
// the authored value directly, with no Camera_Y term.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_InstallPatchedWorldY (a0: u32, d0: u16) clobbers(d0-d1/a0-a1) {
        jbsr    Raster_CopyPatchedTemplate
        move.w  d0, Raster_Water_World_Y
        jbra    Raster_PatchWaterWorldY
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_PatchWaterLine — move the water boundary to screen line d0 by recomputing
// Buf_B's priming arm word (the runtime twin of raster_dsl's arm_at). Cheap enough
// to call every frame from the main loop; the change lands at the next Raster_VBlank
// rewind.
//   d0.w = water screen line, SIGNED — it may legitimately be off-screen in either
//          direction once the line is anchored to a WORLD Y (Raster_PatchWaterWorldY).
//
// OFF-SCREEN IS A SEMANTIC, NOT A CLAMP. The two directions mean opposite things and a
// plain clamp gets one of them wrong:
//   line < MIN  the surface is above the viewport -> the view is entirely BELOW it
//               (fully submerged) -> fire as early as possible so the effect covers the
//               whole screen.
//   line > MAX  the surface is below the viewport -> the effect is not visible at all
//               -> PARK the counter so the program never fires.
// The previous version did neither: it masked the line to 8 bits, so a negative line
// WRAPPED to an arbitrary on-screen row and painted a boundary in the wrong place. That
// was invisible while the line was a build-time-validated constant (the comptime
// constructor's screen-line bounds caught it at compile time) and becomes reachable on
// every vertical scroll the moment the line is world-anchored.
//
// The RUNTIME twin of raster_dsl's arm_at (both spell RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE):
//   arm0 = $8A00 | (line - RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE)
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_PatchWaterLine (d0: i16) clobbers(d0) {
        move.w  d0, Raster_Water_Line
        cmpi.w  #RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE, d0
        blt     .fully_submerged
        cmpi.w  #RASTER_MAX_FIRE_LINE, d0
        bgt     .not_visible
        subi.w  #RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE, d0
        ori.w   #$8A00, d0
        move.w  d0, Raster_Buf_B + WATER_TEMPLATE_ARM0_OFF
        rts
    .fully_submerged:
        // Earliest legal fire. Screen lines 0..MIN-1 are the priming records' own lines
        // and cannot carry the effect, so a fully-submerged view keeps a MIN-line sliver
        // of un-effected pixels at the very top. Authors wanting a seamless full-screen
        // state must set it as settled state instead (Set_VDP_Reg for a register, the
        // section palette for colours), which is a content decision rather than something
        // this proc can fix.
        move.w  #$8A00 | (RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE - RASTER_MIN_FIRE_LINE), d0
        move.w  d0, Raster_Buf_B + WATER_TEMPLATE_ARM0_OFF
        rts
    .not_visible:
        move.w  #RASTER_ARM_PARK, Raster_Buf_B + WATER_TEMPLATE_ARM0_OFF
        rts
}

// -----------------------------------------------
// Raster_PatchWaterWorldY — anchor the boundary to a WORLD y instead of a screen row.
// This is what makes the effect stay on a place in the LEVEL rather than at a fixed
// height on the display: screen_line = Raster_Water_World_Y - Camera_Y(int), recomputed
// per frame. Takes no argument on purpose — the authored world Y lives in RAM so there
// is exactly one place to set it and no way for a caller to pass a stale copy.
// Call once per frame from the main loop, beside the other camera-derived work. The
// subtraction can go negative or past the screen bottom, which is exactly the case
// Raster_PatchWaterLine's two off-screen branches exist to handle.
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc Raster_PatchWaterWorldY () clobbers(d0-d1/a0) {
        // Early-out unless a patched channel is actually live. A later task calls this
        // unconditionally from the engine loop, and a section with no patched effect
        // must not pay for it. No new RAM flag needed: "patched channel live" already
        // has a live-state representative — both install paths (Raster_InstallWater,
        // Raster_InstallPatchedWorldY) point Raster_Active_Buf at Raster_Buf_B, and
        // Raster_VBlank's .copy_program re-points it at Raster_Buf_A whenever a static
        // Pending install is consumed, which is also why patched->static teardown needs
        // nothing explicit here.
        lea     Raster_Buf_B, a0
        cmpa.l  Raster_Active_Buf, a0
        bne.s   .not_patched
        move.w  Raster_Water_World_Y, d0        // the AUTHORED value
        move.w  Camera_Y, d1                    // (Camera_Y).w — 16.16, integer word
        sub.w   d1, d0                          // may go negative; that is meaningful
        jbra    Raster_PatchWaterLine
    .not_patched:
        rts
}
